    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Lately, have you been needing less sleep than usual and still feeling full of energy?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "No, no, I wouldn't say that at all. It's not like I don't need sleep, or that I feel, you know, refreshed after only getting a few hours. Actually, it's quite the opposite in a way. I still need a good seven or eight hours, just like I always have. When I finally do manage to crash—which, let me tell you, is a whole ordeal in itself because my brain just wants to keep going and going, thinking about the lesson plans for the next day, or whether I remembered to send that email to the principal, or if the lawnmower needs a new spark plug—but when I do finally fall asleep, I sleep hard. \n\nAnd when the alarm goes off at 5:30 in the morning, I don't pop out of bed feeling like Superman, you know? I'm tired. I feel my age. My joints ache a little, and I groggily shuffle to the coffee maker just like anybody else. It's just that once the coffee hits, or once I get moving, that motor kicks back on and it just doesn't stop. So it's not a lack of needing sleep, or feeling like I have this magical endless well of energy. I get exhausted, physically and mentally. It's more that my body and my brain refuse to acknowledge that exhaustion during the day. They just keep pushing and pushing, even when I know I should be tired, even when I *want* to be tired and just sit on the couch. So, no, I definitely still need my sleep. If I didn't get it, I don't know how I'd survive the school day, especially with my kids. They require a lot of patience and focus, and if I'm running on empty, well, that's just not fair to them, is it? So I make sure I sleep, it's just the shutting down part that's the real trick.",
