    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Did you have this same kind of trouble sitting still and keeping your mind on one thing when you were a child?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, absolutely. Yes, this isn't some new development, not by a long shot. It's funny you ask that, actually, because I was just talking to my sister about this the other day—we were going through some old boxes of our parents' things since my mom passed a few years back, and we found all these old report cards from when I was in elementary school. And let me tell you, reading those teacher comments was like looking in a mirror, even fifty years later. \n\nMy mother, she used to joke about it all the time. She’d say, \"Tommy\"—that's what they called me back then—\"Tommy, you practically taught the class from your seat.\" And she wasn't exaggerating! I was always, always out of my chair. If the teacher was at the chalkboard, I'd be up wandering over to the pencil sharpener, or going to look out the window, or walking over to see what my buddy was drawing on his desk. I just could not stay glued to the seat. And the talking! Oh, the talking. I was the kid who was always blurting things out, cutting in before the teacher even finished giving the directions for the assignment. I just had this... this urgency, you know? Like whatever was in my head had to come out right that exact second, or I'd burst. \n\nI remember my third-grade teacher, Mrs. Gable—wonderful woman, very patient, which was a blessing for me—she actually put a little strip of masking tape on the floor around my desk. It was my \"boundary.\" But even then, I'd be leaning over the tape, stretching as far as I could go without technically stepping out of it, just to see what was happening on the other side of the room. So, yes, this whole... this engine constantly running, this inability to just sit and be still, it's been part of who I am for as long as I can remember. Long before I became a teacher myself, long before I got married or had any of the responsibilities I have now... long before any recent family changes or stress. It's just... it's just me. It’s always been me, from the very beginning. It's just getting so exhausting to keep up with it now at my age.",
