    {
      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "Do you tend to worry excessively about everyday things, even when there isn’t a clear reason to?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Well, no, I wouldn't use the word worry. Not at all. I mean, sure, I think about things constantly, like I was just saying, but I'm not sitting around wringing my hands or feeling anxious about it. It's actually funny you use that word, because people sometimes look at me—my wife, for instance, or even some of the younger teachers in the special ed department, the ones who are fresh out of college and still trying to figure out how to manage a classroom—they'll see me pacing around the teacher's lounge, muttering to myself about the schedule for the upcoming IEP meetings, or checking my pockets three times to make sure I have my keys, and they probably think, 'Oh, Tom's really stressed out, he's really worried about something.' But I'm really not! \n\nWorry, at least to me, implies some kind of fear, right? Like you're afraid something bad is going to happen if you don't fix it. I don't have that at all. If I'm thinking about... oh, I don't know, the gutters needing cleaning, I'm not sitting there worried that the house is going to flood and wash away in the next rainstorm. I'm just thinking, 'Okay, the gutters need cleaning, which means I gotta get the tall ladder, but the tall ladder is behind the lawnmower in the shed, and the shed needs a new padlock because the old one is rusting, so I should probably go to the hardware store, and hey, while I'm there, they might still have that sale on mulch...' \n\nYou see what I mean? It's... it's a to-do list. It's a very loud, very fast to-do list that's running on a loop at a hundred miles an hour, but it's not driven by anxiety or dread. It's just logistics. It's just my brain trying to organize a million little puzzle pieces all at the",
